# AWS deployment — command log

A chronological log of every AWS/eksctl/kubectl command run while deploying Code
Runtime to EKS, with purpose and outcome. Region: **ap-southeast-2**, account
**768054003064**. Append new commands as they run.

---

## Phase 0 — tooling (local)
```bash
brew install awscli eksctl helm        # aws 2.35.9, eksctl 0.227.0, helm v4.2.2
```

## Phase 1 — identity & discovery (read-only)
```bash
# Who am I / which account
aws sts get-caller-identity
#   → Account 768054003064, Arn arn:aws:iam::768054003064:root   (⚠ root — replace with IAM admin, delete root keys after)

aws configure get region
#   → ap-southeast-2

# Confirm the SQS job queue is reachable
aws sqs get-queue-attributes \
  --queue-url https://sqs.ap-southeast-2.amazonaws.com/768054003064/code-submission-processing-queue \
  --attribute-names ApproximateNumberOfMessages QueueArn --region ap-southeast-2
#   → ApproximateNumberOfMessages 0, ARN arn:aws:sqs:ap-southeast-2:768054003064:code-submission-processing-queue

# Where does the database live? (EKS must share its VPC to reach it privately)
aws rds describe-db-instances --region ap-southeast-2 \
  --query 'DBInstances[].{id:DBInstanceIdentifier,engine:Engine,status:DBInstanceStatus,endpoint:Endpoint.Address,vpc:DBSubnetGroup.VpcId,public:PubliclyAccessible}'
#   → coderuntime-prod (mysql, available) endpoint coderuntime-prod.cziyoeg0oioy.ap-southeast-2.rds.amazonaws.com
#     vpc-0c19448c00a89ccb8, PubliclyAccessible=false

aws rds describe-db-clusters --region ap-southeast-2 \
  --query 'DBClusters[].{id:DBClusterIdentifier,status:Status,endpoint:Endpoint}'
#   → database-1 (aurora) database-1.cluster-cziyoeg0oioy.ap-southeast-2.rds.amazonaws.com

aws ec2 describe-vpcs --region ap-southeast-2 \
  --query 'Vpcs[].{id:VpcId,cidr:CidrBlock,default:IsDefault}'
#   → ONLY vpc-0c19448c00a89ccb8 (172.31.0.0/16, default)  ← DB lives here

aws ec2 describe-subnets --region ap-southeast-2 \
  --filters Name=vpc-id,Values=vpc-0c19448c00a89ccb8 \
  --query 'Subnets[].{id:SubnetId,az:AvailabilityZone,cidr:CidrBlock,public:MapPublicIpOnLaunch}'
#   → subnet-09b8927e9af369a62 (2a, 172.31.0.0/20, public)
#     subnet-09c936e6e575c677a (2b, 172.31.32.0/20, public)
#     subnet-0bdff67df02afc941 (2c, 172.31.16.0/20, public)

aws rds describe-db-instances --region ap-southeast-2 --db-instance-identifier coderuntime-prod \
  --query 'DBInstances[0].VpcSecurityGroups[].VpcSecurityGroupId' --output text
#   → sg-089caf4f4cdf7be6e   (RDS security group — must allow 3306 from EKS nodes)
```

### Decisions from Phase 1
- **EKS goes in the existing default VPC** `vpc-0c19448c00a89ccb8` using the 3 public
  subnets, so pods reach the private RDS on the same network (no VPC peering).
- After the cluster exists, **allow 3306 on the RDS SG (`sg-089caf4f4cdf7be6e`)
  from the EKS node security group** (or the VPC CIDR 172.31.0.0/16).

