## Phase 2c — nodegroup blocked by Free-Tier restriction
```bash
# Symptom: nodegroup CREATE failed, 0 EC2 instances launched.
aws autoscaling describe-scaling-activities --auto-scaling-group-name <eks-system-asg> --region ap-southeast-2
#   → "The specified instance type is not eligible for Free Tier."
# This account is restricted to FREE-TIER-ELIGIBLE instance types only.
aws ec2 describe-instance-types --region ap-southeast-2 \
  --filters Name=free-tier-eligible,Values=true \
  --query 'InstanceTypes[].{type:InstanceType,vcpu:VCpuInfo.DefaultVCpus,memMiB:MemoryInfo.SizeInMiB}'
#   → t3.micro/small, t4g.micro/small, c7i-flex.large(4GB), m7i-flex.large(8GB)
# FIX: set system nodegroup instanceType: m7i-flex.large (free-tier eligible, 8GB).
#      (Upgrade account to a paid plan for m6i.large + larger Karpenter nodes.)
eksctl delete nodegroup --cluster coderuntime --name system --region ap-southeast-2 --wait
eksctl create nodegroup -f cluster.yaml      # with m7i-flex.large
```

## How the nodegroup failure was diagnosed (the trail)
Work from the symptom inward: eksctl error → CloudFormation → EKS nodegroup
health → EC2 instances → Auto Scaling Group activity (the real cause lives here).

```bash
# 1. eksctl said the cluster/nodegroup stack failed — get the CFN failure reason.
#    (First failure was a race:)
aws cloudformation describe-stack-events --region ap-southeast-2 \
  --stack-name eksctl-coderuntime-nodegroup-system \
  --query 'StackEvents[?ResourceStatus==`CREATE_FAILED`].[LogicalResourceId,ResourceStatusReason]' --output text
#   → "Cluster 'coderuntime' is not in ACTIVE status"  (nodegroup raced cluster UPDATING)

# 2. After retry it timed out ("exceeded max wait"). Check the nodegroup's own health:
aws eks describe-nodegroup --cluster-name coderuntime --nodegroup-name system \
  --region ap-southeast-2 --query 'nodegroup.{status:status,health:health.issues}'
#   → status CREATING, health [] (no node-join errors → nodes never even launched)

# 3. Did any EC2 instances actually launch for this nodegroup?
aws ec2 describe-instances --region ap-southeast-2 \
  --filters "Name=tag:eks:nodegroup-name,Values=system" "Name=instance-state-name,Values=running,pending" \
  --query 'Reservations[].Instances[].{id:InstanceId,state:State.Name}'
#   → []  (ZERO instances — the ASG can't launch them → check ASG activity)

# 4. Find the nodegroup's Auto Scaling Group, then read its scaling activities —
#    THIS is where EC2 launch errors surface.
ASG=$(aws autoscaling describe-auto-scaling-groups --region ap-southeast-2 \
  --query "AutoScalingGroups[?contains(Tags[?Key=='eks:nodegroup-name'].Value | [0],'system')].AutoScalingGroupName | [0]" --output text)
aws autoscaling describe-scaling-activities --region ap-southeast-2 \
  --auto-scaling-group-name "$ASG" --max-records 4 \
  --query 'Activities[].{status:StatusCode,cause:StatusMessage}'
#   → "Could not launch On-Demand Instances ... not eligible for Free Tier."  ← ROOT CAUSE

# 5. Rule out a quota red-herring, then list what IS allowed.
aws service-quotas get-service-quota --region ap-southeast-2 \
  --service-code ec2 --quota-code L-1216C47A   # On-Demand Standard vCPUs → 16 (fine, not the issue)
aws ec2 describe-instance-types --region ap-southeast-2 \
  --filters Name=free-tier-eligible,Values=true \
  --query 'InstanceTypes[].{type:InstanceType,vcpu:VCpuInfo.DefaultVCpus,memMiB:MemoryInfo.SizeInMiB}'
#   → m7i-flex.large (8GB) chosen.
```
**Lesson:** for "node won't join / nodegroup CREATE failed", the EC2 *launch* error is
not in eksctl/CFN/EKS — it's in **ASG scaling activities**. Always drill: CFN events →
nodegroup health → EC2 instances present? → ASG activities.

