## Phase 6 — worker (DinD) + KEDA on EKS
```bash
helm upgrade --install keda kedacore/keda -n keda --set serviceAccount.operator.name=keda-operator
#   (SA pre-created by eksctl → add Helm ownership labels/annotations so Helm adopts it)
kubectl apply -f eks/keda-scaledobject-eks.yaml      # KEDA SQS scaler, IRSA (podIdentity)
# Worker on AL2023 (containerd, no host docker.sock) → DinD sidecar over TCP:
#   - add docker:27-dind sidecar (privileged, runAsUser 0) listening tcp://0.0.0.0:2375
#   - worker env DOCKER_HOST=tcp://localhost:2375  (SDK client.FromEnv reads DOCKER_HOST)
#   - emptyDir for /var/lib/docker + sandbox; drop hostPath docker-socket
kubectl -n coderuntime apply -f /tmp/worker-eks.json
# Removed imagePullSecrets (ECR pulls use node IAM, not a pull secret).
```
RESULT: worker 2/2 (worker+dind), dind reachable, "worker consuming from sqs" via IRSA,
KEDA ScaledObject READY (scales worker on SQS backlog). Sandbox EXECUTION still needs the
34 code-runtime-* images pushed to ECR + dind ECR auth (prewarm shows pull-access-denied).

## Phase 7 — make sandbox EXECUTION work from private ECR (the full fix)
Approach chosen: "Custom images to private ECR + dind registry auth" (full 40-language coverage).
The runtime images stay referenced locally by their bare tag `code-runtime-<lang>:latest`; the
worker's ImagePool transparently pulls them from ECR and the dind daemon executes them.

### 7a. Build + push runtime images to ECR (amd64, the node arch)
```bash
REGION=ap-southeast-2; ACCOUNT=768054003064
REGISTRY=$ACCOUNT.dkr.ecr.$REGION.amazonaws.com
aws ecr get-login-password --region $REGION | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin $REGISTRY
# one repo per language, then build+push (linux/amd64 to match m7i-flex.large nodes):
for l in <langs>; do
  aws ecr describe-repositories --repository-names code-runtime-$l --region $REGION >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || aws ecr create-repository --repository-name code-runtime-$l --region $REGION >/dev/null
  docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64 -t $REGISTRY/code-runtime-$l:latest --push runtime-images/$l
done
# helper: scripts/build-push-runtime-ecr.sh  (LANGS="bash golang nodejs ..." bash scripts/build-push-runtime-ecr.sh)
```

### 7b. BUG #1 — registry env var name (pull ref was un-prefixed)
Symptom: worker log `{"msg":"pulling docker image","image":"code-runtime-bash:latest","ref":"code-runtime-bash:latest"}`
— `ref` NOT prefixed with the ECR registry, so `resolvePullRef` saw an empty `p.registry`
→ `pull access denied for code-runtime-bash`.
Root cause: the Viper config field is `DockerConfig.Registry` (`mapstructure:"registry"` under the
`docker` section), so the env keys MUST be `CODERUNTIME_DOCKER_REGISTRY` / `CODERUNTIME_DOCKER_REGISTRY_AUTH`.
I had used `CODERUNTIME_REGISTRY` / `CODERUNTIME_REGISTRY_AUTH` (missing the `DOCKER_` segment).
Fix — recreate the secret with the correct keys and wire it via envFrom on the worker:
```bash
TOKEN=$(aws ecr get-login-password --region ap-southeast-2)
XAUTH=$(python3 -c "import json,base64,sys;print(base64.b64encode(json.dumps({'username':'AWS','password':sys.argv[1],'serveraddress':sys.argv[2]}).encode()).decode())" "$TOKEN" "$REGISTRY")
kubectl -n coderuntime create secret generic ecr-registry-auth \
  --from-literal=CODERUNTIME_DOCKER_REGISTRY="$REGISTRY" \
  --from-literal=CODERUNTIME_DOCKER_REGISTRY_AUTH="$XAUTH" \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
kubectl -n coderuntime rollout restart deployment/worker
```
NOTE: the X-Registry-Auth token (ECR password) expires ~12h — re-run the above to refresh.
RESULT: prewarm/pull ref now correctly resolves to
`768054003064.dkr.ecr.ap-southeast-2.amazonaws.com/code-runtime-<lang>:latest`.

### 7c. BUG #2 — DinD bind-mount source not visible to the daemon
Symptom: `invalid mount config for type "bind": bind source path does not exist: /tmp/sandbox/<uuid>`.
Root cause: the worker writes per-execution source files to its host workdir and bind-mounts that
dir into the sandbox container — but the BIND IS CREATED BY THE DIND DAEMON, which has its own
filesystem. The dir existed in the worker container, not in dind.
First attempt: share an emptyDir named `sandbox` and mount it at `/tmp/sandbox` in BOTH worker and
dind. STILL FAILED — `/proc/mounts` in dind showed `/tmp/sandbox` (the shared emptyDir) mounted
FIRST, then a `tmpfs` mounted over `/tmp` (docker:dind sets this up), SHADOWING `/tmp/sandbox`.
Verified: a file written in worker's /tmp/sandbox was invisible in dind's /tmp/sandbox.
Fix: move the host sandbox workdir OUTSIDE `/tmp` so nothing shadows it.
  - worker env `CODERUNTIME_WORKER_TMP_DIR=/sandbox-host` (config key `worker.tmp_dir`; this is the
    host base dir the sandbox bind-mounts FROM — NOT `docker.work_dir`, which is the in-container target).
  - mount the shared `sandbox` emptyDir at `/sandbox-host` in BOTH worker and dind containers.
```bash
kubectl -n coderuntime patch deployment worker --type=json -p='[
  {"op":"replace","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/volumeMounts/0/mountPath","value":"/sandbox-host"},
  {"op":"replace","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/1/volumeMounts/1/mountPath","value":"/sandbox-host"},
  {"op":"add","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/env/-","value":{"name":"CODERUNTIME_WORKER_TMP_DIR","value":"/sandbox-host"}}
]'
# verify cross-container share:
POD=$(kubectl -n coderuntime get pod -l app.kubernetes.io/name=worker -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
kubectl -n coderuntime exec $POD -c worker -- sh -c 'mkdir -p /sandbox-host/probe && echo ok > /sandbox-host/probe/f.txt'
kubectl -n coderuntime exec $POD -c dind   -- cat /sandbox-host/probe/f.txt   # -> ok  (DIND-SEES-FILE)
```
(worker-dind-patch.yaml updated to add the dind `sandbox` mount as the source of truth.)

### 7d. API auth for testing
- API key header is `X-Auth-Token` (sha256(rawkey) looked up in api_keys); JWT via `Authorization: Bearer`.
- Login (seeded user): `POST /auth/token {"email":"dev@coderuntime.io","password":"Admin123!"}` -> access_token.
- Routes are at ROOT (`/submissions`, `/languages`, ...). The `/code-runtime` prefix is added by the
  ingress/gateway, not the app. Submissions require numeric `language_id` (not `language`).

